# Deligne's 1968 revision of Tate's formulary.
C(812,('H63-03',[16,17],'12-63'),'courbes de genre1 ... section dans le lieu lisse',
 'For a proper flat finitely presented family of geometrically integral reduced curves of arithmetic genus1 with a section e in the smooth locus, e is an effective Cartier divisor. For n>0, R¹p*O(ne)=0 and p*O(ne) is locally free of rank n, compatible with arbitrary base change; p*O=p*O(e)=OS. The graded pieces for n≥2 are ω^−n, where ω=e*Ω¹C/S.',
 'proof sketched','Fiberwise Riemann–Roch and Serre duality: a negative-degree invertible sheaf has no sections on a geometrically integral proper curve; cohomology/base change gives the family statement. The n=1 quotient is zero and must not be confused with the n≥2 formula.',
 'B','Deligne1968 preprint M/68/28, then Deligne1975 §1, is the same authored text; the proof uses standard cohomology and base change.','IHÉS preprint1968; publication1975.','Established for integral fibers; arbitrary reducible generalized elliptic curves require a modified formulation.',['Deligne1968','Deligne1975','EGAIII'],author='Pierre Deligne',family='DELIGNE')
C(813,('H63-03',[17,18],'55-96'),'Ωreg; p*ω→Ωreg',
 'Such genus-one plane-cubic fibers are Gorenstein; their relative dualizing sheaf is canonically the pullback of ω after normalization along e. Evaluation p*p*Ωreg→Ωreg is an isomorphism because a nonzero regular differential on an integral arithmetic-genus-one curve has no zero. The notation Rp! and the indexing in the transcription require its cohomological convention to be checked, not a forced replacement by an ordinary cotangent sheaf at a node.',
 'open on the pages: marginal verification request','The fiberwise degree-zero argument and base change prove the evaluation assertion under C-SA16-812 hypotheses; no equality with the singular Kähler differential sheaf is used.',
 'B','Deligne1975 §1 and Grothendieck duality supply the family dualizing interpretation.','1968 circulation;1975 print.','Established in the Gorenstein/dualizing formulation; the transcription’s derived-degree convention remains an ink question.',['Deligne1975','EGAIII'],author='Pierre Deligne',family='DELIGNE')
C(814,('H63-03',[18,19],'68-115'),'x,y,1 ... Weierstrass',
 'Locally trivializing ω and choosing normalized bases in p*O(2e),p*O(3e) produces x,y with pole orders2,3, a unique relation y²+a1xy+a3y=x³+a2x²+a4x+a6, and a plane-cubic embedding from O(3e). The distinguished point is the flex at infinity. Different choices are related by the admissible coordinate group.',
 'proof sketched','Riemann–Roch dimensions1,2,3,6 in degrees1,2,3,6 force the unique relation after monomial normalization. Homogenizing the equation makes the line at infinity meet at e with multiplicity3.',
 'B','Deligne1968/1975 §1 is the published construction.','1968/1975.','Established locally on the base; global x,y require line-bundle trivializations.',['Deligne1968','Deligne1975'],author='Pierre Deligne',family='DELIGNE')
C(815,('H63-03',[19,20,21],'117-194'),'12d6=c6+b2c4; −12d8=c4²+b2c6',
 'The auxiliary invariants d6=b2b4−18b6 and d8=b2²b4−48b4²+18b2b6 satisfy the displayed identities and Δ=b4³−27b6²+b8(36b4−b2²). Coordinate-shift formulas follow from the Weierstrass substitution; the discriminant of the completed-square cubic is16Δ.',
 'open on the pages at the exponent check; otherwise stated','All polynomial identities have zero exact residual in c63_algebra.py. The exponent b2² in the second discriminant expression is correct. The alternate a4 formula requires −F_x rather than −F.',
 'E','These are elementary consequences of the classical integral invariant formulas, also present in Deligne1975 §1.','Classical; formulary1968/1975.','Established as polynomial identities overZ.',['Deligne1975'],author='Pierre Deligne',family='DELIGNE')
C(816,('H63-03',[21,22],'210-257'),'Spec Z[1/6][g2,g3]',
 'OverZ[1/6], integral reduced genus-one curves with smooth marked origin and a nowhere-zero invariant differential are represented by the affine (g2,g3)-plane via Y²=4X³−g2X−g3. Smooth elliptic curves are its open Δ≠0; the nodal and cuspidal points remain in the larger genus-one moduli problem. The marking is[0:1:0] and ω=dX/Y.',
 'proof sketched with marginal question at the canonical section','Completing square and eliminating the quadratic term gives the unique normalized equation, verified by the coordinate formulas. At the marked point ∂F/∂Z≠0, including singular finite fibers.',
 'B','Deligne1975 §1 states this rigidified representability and the normal forms.','1968 preprint;1975 publication.','Established for the stated enlarged genus-one functor; it is not the smooth elliptic moduli space unless Δ is inverted.',['Deligne1975'],author='Pierre Deligne',family='DELIGNE')
C(817,('H63-03',[22,23],'259-316'),'d6=0; a2a4=9a6',
 'When2 and c4 are invertible, the equation can be uniquely gauged to a1=a3=0 and d6=0 after choosing the differential. Then a2a4=9a6, a2c4=−c6/4, a4c4²=−36Δ, a6c4³=c6Δ; these equations describe the ordinary c4-open normal form.',
 'open on the pages at uniqueness/existence; formulas given','Exact substitution into the invariant definitions verifies all four identities in c63_more_algebra.py. The translation changes d6 by a unit multiple of c4, so the required r is unique when c4 is invertible.',
 'B','Deligne1975 §1 publishes the same gauge; it is not a global gauge across c4=0.','1968/1975.','Established with2,c4 units and a chosen differential.',['Deligne1975'],author='Pierre Deligne',family='DELIGNE')
C(818,('H63-03/H63-04',[24,25],'H63-03:318-343;H63-04:6-41'),'en caractéristique2 ... en caractéristique3',
 'On the ordinary c4-open in characteristic2, a normalized equation is y²+xy=x³+a2x²+a6, with c4=c6=1 and Δ=a6; residual changes are Artin–Schreier changes in a2. Over an algebraically closed field the supersingular models are y²+y=x³ in characteristic2 and y²=x³−x in characteristic3. The respective discriminants are a3⁴ and−a4³ in the unnormalized special forms.',
 'stated without proof; normalizations explained','Direct reduction of the invariant polynomials modulo2 and3 and coordinate changes is included in c63_more_algebra.py. Algebraic closedness/separable extensions are needed to solve the normalization equations.',
 'E','Deligne1975 §§1–2 and Tate’s small-characteristic formulary give these normal forms.','Classical forms;1968/1975 publication of the text.','Established with field and trivialization hypotheses; ordinary and supersingular branches must be kept separate.',['Deligne1975','Tate1975'],author='Pierre Deligne',family='DELIGNE')
C(819,('H63-04',[26],'50-60'),'Δ≠0; Δ=0,c4≠0; c4=Δ=0',
 'Over a field, a Weierstrass cubic with its marked smooth point is smooth iffΔ≠0, nodal iffΔ=0 andc4≠0, and cuspidal iffΔ=c4=0. The nodal assertion is geometric; its torus can be nonsplit over the ground field.',
 'stated without proof','Jacobian and tangent-cone classification reduce to the completed-square cubic when2≠0; characteristic2 is checked in its normal forms. In particular discriminant vanishing alone does not distinguish node from cusp.',
 'E','Tate1975 §§1,3 and Deligne1975 §1.','Known before these notes.','Established.',['Tate1975','Deligne1975'],author='Pierre Deligne',family='DELIGNE')
C(820,('H63-04',[27,28,29],'71-127'),'Isom ... fini non ramifié ... j=j′',
 'For pointed genus-one curves without additive fibers (smooth or nodal), the Isom functor is finite unramified. Equal j makes its projection surjective on the underlying topological spaces; on the open avoiding0 and1728 it is finite étale of rank2. The page does not claim flatness of Isom at the special j-values. In characteristic2 the ordinary twisting equation is Artin–Schreier; when2 is invertible the generic twist is quadratic.',
 'proof sketched','The explicit coordinate equations prove finiteness and the absence of infinitesimal origin-preserving automorphisms. The ordinary equation’s a2x term in the transcription must be a2x².',
 'B','Deligne1975 §2 develops these isomorphism functors and the coarse j-invariant.','1968/1975.','Established as written, including nodal fibers. An fppf-isomorphism assertion over all special-j nonreduced bases would be stronger and false: y²=x³+εx+1 overC[ε]/ε² has j=0 but is not a twist of the constant j0 curve.',['Deligne1975'],author='Pierre Deligne',family='DELIGNE')
C(821,('H63-04',[30,31,32],'152-221'),'±1; μ6; μ4; structure?',
 'Over an algebraically closed field, the pointed automorphism group has order2 generically; outside characteristic2,3 it is μ6 atj0 andμ4 atj1728; at the supersingular point in characteristic3 it is C3⋊C4 of order12; in characteristic2 it is SL2(F3) of order24 with quaternion kernelQ8 on the differential character. Over disconnected bases, a global section of the constant order2 group can choose signs separately on components, so there are more than two global automorphisms.',
 'false as stated for a literal two-element set over arbitrary disconnected bases; the margin raises the objection','Counterexample: overS=Spec(C×C), take the same generic-j elliptic curve on both components. The map(+1,−1) is neither global+1 nor global−1. Finite group computations in c63_algebra.py and c63_more_algebra.py check the small-characteristic groups.',
 'E','The automorphism classification is classical and is made explicit in Deligne1975 §2. The correct relative formulation is a group scheme/functor, not a fixed two-element set of sections.','Known before publication1975.','Established after this base-sensitive correction; the displayed composition formula has a separate variable alarm.',['Deligne1975'],author='Pierre Deligne',family='DELIGNE')
C(822,('H63-04',[32,33,34],'226-267'),'Z[c4,c6,Δ]/(c4³−c6²−1728Δ)',
 'The ring of integral level-one modular forms, regular at the cusp, is Z[c4,c6,Δ]/(c4³−c6²−1728Δ), with weights4,6,12. In characteristics2 and3 the corresponding rings are F2[a1,Δ] andF3[b2,Δ], weights1,12 and2,12. Removing the cuspidal/additive locus in the smooth Weierstrass atlas does not change sections when its complement has codimension≥2 and the atlas is normal. Inverting Δ instead gives weakly holomorphic forms and a different ring.',
 'proof sketched; codimension extension explicitly questioned','The invariant relation is checked exactly; the Hartogs extension argument is a separate normality/codimension proof. The mod3 b2 calculation cannot be assigned toF2 as one marginal label appears to do.',
 'B','Deligne1975 §3 and Katz1973 §§1–2 give integral modular forms and exceptional-characteristic behavior.','Deligne preprint1968, print1975; Katz1973.','Established with the stated regularity convention.',['Deligne1975','Katz1973'],author='Pierre Deligne',family='MODFORMS')
C(823,('H63-04',[34],'269-281'),'interprétation ... Hasse–Witt ... relèvement canonique',
 'The invariants a1 in characteristic2 and b2 in characteristic3 are the respective Hasse invariants as sections ofω^(p−1); this interpretation is valid over arbitrary characteristic-p bases using Frobenius/Verschiebung, without choosing a perfect-field inverse Frobenius. An ordinary elliptic curve over a perfect field has its Serre–Tate canonical lift to Witt vectors. A universal canonical lift for supersingular curves, or a single elementary formula for every base, is not asserted by this theorem.',
 'open on the pages','Characteristic2/3 Hasse polynomials checked in c63_more_algebra.py; the canonical-lift existence and uniqueness are used from Serre–Tate theory, not claimed proved by coefficient calculations.',
 'B','Katz1973 §2.0 defines the Hasse invariant functorially; Serre–Tate deformation theory establishes canonical lifts on the ordinary locus.','Serre–Tate theorycirculated1960s; Katz1973 exposition.','The intrinsic interpretation is settled; explicit universal formulas require specifying an ordinary locus, coordinate and convergence domain.',['Katz1973','Katz1981SerreTate'],author='Pierre Deligne / annotating hand not independently identified',family='MODFORMS')

# Atkin papers: reported computations, conjectures and their exact later quantifiers.
C(824,('H63-05',[42],'45-87'),'24n−1 divisible par5^a7^b11^c',
 'The original Ramanujan assertion v5(p(n))≥a,v7(p(n))≥b,v11(p(n))≥c when24n−1 is divisible by5^a7^b11^c is false in the7-part. The corrected7 exponent for b≥1 is floor((b+2)/2); b=0 imposes no7-divisibility. The5- and11-parts retain their stated exponents.',
 'corrected by himself later: the printed exposition gives the corrected7 exponent after the original conjecture','c63_numerics.py computes p(243),24·243−1=7³·17 and its7-adic valuation2, giving a fully checked counterexample to exponent3. The pentagonal-number recurrence is exact integer arithmetic.',
 'E','Watson proved the5/7 refinements; Atkin proved the11-power theorem. The copied exposition reports these earlier results; GarvanShomanov2025 supplies checked historical references.','Watson1938; Atkin1967; report circulated atBlaricum1966 and printed1968.','Established in the corrected form.',['Atkin1968','GarvanShomanov2025','Atkin1967_11'],author='A. O. L. Atkin reporting Ramanujan/Watson',family='ATKINPART')
C(825,('H63-05',[43],'92-124'),'J=j−744 ... c(n)',
 'Let j−744=q^−1+Σn≥1 c(n)q^n. The displayed initial coefficients and Lehner divisibility bounds are v2(c(n))≥3a+8 if2^a|n witha≥1; v3≥2b+3 forb≥1; v5≥c+1 forc≥1; v7≥d ford≥1; v11≥e fore≥1. The hypotheses on positive exponents matter; these formulas do not impose all factors on every coefficient.',
 'stated without proof','Exact q-series from E4³/Δ and integer recurrence are recomputed in c63_numerics.py; finite checked coefficient ranges are recorded and are not proofs of the all-n divisibility theorem.',
 'E','The exposition attributes these congruences toLehner; Guerzhoy2010 introduction restates the normalized divisibility setting.','Lehner results predating the1966/1968 report.','Established classical congruences; finite numerical checks are labeled as such.',['Atkin1968','Guerzhoy2010'],author='A. O. L. Atkin reporting Lehner',family='ATKINJ')
C(826,('H63-05',[44,45],'144-199'),'c(13²n)≡8c(13n); −c(13n)≡τ(n)',
 'Modulo13, U13(j−744) is−Δ, so c(13²n)≡8c(13n) and −c(13n)≡τ(n). The Ramanujan τ coefficients satisfy τ(mn)=τ(m)τ(n) for coprime m,n and τ(pn)=τ(p)τ(n)−p11τ(n/p), with the last term0 ifp∤n. These exact Hecke identities explain the congruence recurrence and propagate a vanishing at a prime to multiples coprime to that prime.',
 'stated without proof','c63_numerics.py recomputes Δ,j and the modulo13 relations on the recorded range; its symbolic Hecke recurrence handles the all-n implication once the modular-form identity is given.',
 'E','Newman’s congruence is explicitly identified in Guerzhoy2006, corollary1 proof and eq(5). The τ Hecke relations go back toMordell and Hecke.','Mordell1917/1919 date in the archival wording retained; exact cited edition must be checked. Newman before1966; Guerzhoy2006.','Established. A finite τ table does not prove that τ has no zero.',['Atkin1968','Guerzhoy2006'],author='A. O. L. Atkin',family='ATKINJ')
C(827,('H63-05',[46,47],'209-267'),'R13^α ... M13^α',
 'For j coefficients, the13-adic recurrence R and normalized Hecke multiplicativity M are asserted for everyα≥1 in the later portion of this exposition. The denominator c(13^α) is a13-adic unit, so tα(n)=c(13^αn)/c(13^α) is well-defined modulo13^α. The paper separately reports finite computations up toα8 and bounds on primes/indices; those are evidence, not the universal theorem.',
 'stated without proof: proof announced for13; finite experiments described separately','Exact moderate-index congruences and the displayed normalized table are addressed by c63_numerics.py and c63_u13.py. Each output records its modulus and tested index set; the all-α assertion is attributed to the exact later theorem.',
 'B','Guerzhoy2006 p2 states that the sharp choice M(α)=α is known forj atp13 fromKoike/Katz; this is more precise than the eventual-iteration theorem for all four primes.','Atkin–OBrien1967 and Atkin1968; Koike/Katz1970s; explicit later comparison2006.','The13 case is established. Do not infer the sharp bound for17,19,23 from the same citation.',['Atkin1968','AtkinOBrien1967','Guerzhoy2006','Katz1973'],author='A. O. L. Atkin',family='ATKINJ')
C(828,('H63-05',[47,48],'269-290'),'densité ... p((13^αN+1)/24)',
 'A Hecke congruence with a vanishing eigenvalue modulo13^α at a prime propagates zero coefficients along the stated prime divisibility progression, giving a positive-density set of indices. For partitions the half-integral-weight Hecke relation involves p², a Legendre symbol and p^−2,p^−3 factors; it cannot be replaced by the integral-weight j relation. The deleted universal qualifier in the manuscript does not establish the all-α partition conjecture.',
 'proof sketched for the density implication; the all-α partition assertion remains a conjectural/deleted scope','Propagation is re-derived algebraically from the Hecke recurrence with the coprimality condition. The13-adic partition formula is numerically tested in c63_numerics.py in explicit finite ranges.',
 'B','GarvanShomanov2025 proves the exact Atkin–OBrien partition Hecke conjecture for everyα≥1 and every auxiliary primep≥5,p≠13 (Conjecture1.2, proved in§4); publication isAdvances inMath490(2026)110840. Earlier Folsom–Kent–Ono/Boylan–Webb results have deeper-iteration bounds.','Preprint15April2025; publication2026, distinct fromAtkin–OBrien1967.','The exact13-power partition conjecture is settled as ofNovember2026. This does not settle every higher-prime speculation on these pages.',['Atkin1968','AtkinOBrien1967','GarvanShomanov2025','GarvanShomanov2026'],author='A. O. L. Atkin',family='ATKINPART')
C(829,('H63-05/H63-06',[48,49,50,51],'H63-05:285-290;H63-06:7-106'),'17,19,23 ... α≤5 ... 29',
 'The pages report finite-level analogues for17,19,23 and conjecture uniform normalized Hecke congruences asα grows. At29 the two-dimensional U29 action is represented by [[26,3],[14,21]] in the displayed ordered basis; its eigenvectors requireF29² and the slope equation is μ²−8μ+5=0. At31 two components and at37 three are suggested; the dimension floor(p/12) is a heuristic requiring a precise modular-space definition.',
 'open on the pages beyond the explicitly tested finite ranges','c63_algebra.py checks the matrix, irreducibility of the slope polynomial, determinant11 and M840=I overF29. It therefore proves the reported period841=29² for one U iterate, under this matrix model. It does not prove all higher-power lifting claims.',
 'C','Guerzhoy2006 Thm1 establishes eventual congruence to a Hecke eigenform forp13,17,19,23 with rationality/pole hypotheses and quantifiers∀α∃M(α)∀m≥M. His paper explicitly does not give the archival sharp M(α)=α for the latter three. Higher-rank ordinary spaces explain why29/31 do not reduce to one eigenline.','2006; later generalp-adic theory is earlier inSerre1973/Katz1973/Hida.','The eventual statement is proved; the exact sharp thresholds and higher-prime formulation must remain separate records.',['Guerzhoy2006','Serre1973','Katz1973'],author='A. O. L. Atkin',family='ATKINJ')
C(830,('H63-06',[52,53],'117-154'),'Δ² ... √144169 ... q=5',
 'The weight24 cusp space has dimension2 and its normalized Hecke eigenforms have coefficient fieldQ(√144169). The discussion uses this as an analogy for congruence eigencomponents. Forp5 the stated normalized multiplicativity is modulo5^(3α), a sharper slope-dependent modulus than simply5^α. The p2,3,7 cases andp11 reservations are individually stated, not a theorem for everyprime.',
 'stated without proof; p11 caveat remains','c63_numerics.py builds the weight24 basisΔE4³ andΔ², computesT2, its characteristic polynomial/discriminant and the quadratic coefficient field. The normalized small-prime checks are finite and record denominators before modular inversion.',
 'B','Guerzhoy2010 proves the small-prime Atkin conjecture with a quantitative congruence modulus; the eigenfield is classical Hecke theory. Its exact slope bounds are kept distinct from the transcription’s initial observations.','Classical weight24 calculation; Guerzhoy preprint2008, publication2010.','Established in the precisely stated later theorem; no blanket all-prime generalization.',['Atkin1968','Guerzhoy2010'],author='A. O. L. Atkin',family='ATKINJ')
C(831,('H63-06',[54,55],'170-213'),'Euler ... N^(3/2) ...',
 'Euler’s pentagonal-number recurrence computes all partitions throughN inO(N^(3/2)) arithmetic operations usingO(N) stored coefficients; multiplying by a dense series naively costsO(N²). The claimed prime-index Hecke testing cost depends on the chosen coefficient bounds and prime loops. The quoted machine timings and memory use are historical reports, not mathematical complexity bounds.',
 'proof sketched for recurrence; implementation timings reported','c63_numerics.py implements and counts pentagonal recurrence additions. Arithmetic-operation counts are separated from bit complexity, since coefficient lengths grow withN. Historical hardware timings cannot be reproduced from the unspecified original program.',
 'E','The Euler/Jacobi product identities are classical; the reported1960s computations are contextual evidence. No claim of modern optimality is made.','Classical identities; machine reports1962–1964/1966.','The recurrence and operation count are valid; literal old hardware performance is not an independently verified theorem.',['Atkin1968','DLMF27Partitions'],author='A. O. L. Atkin',family='ATKINPART')
C(832,('H63-07',[57],'16-26'),'p(206839n+2623)≡0 (mod17)',
 'The displayed partition progression has modulus206839 and offset2623 and is asserted divisible by17 for every nonnegative integern. This is a specific higher-prime partition congruence, not the original Ramanujan progressionp(17n+a).',
 'stated without proof','c63_numerics.py verifies at leastn=0 by an exact partition recurrence, records the precise range, and tests additionaln if the separate fast recurrence run reaches them. The universal claim is checked against Atkin’s published congruence source.',
 'B','Atkin’s1967 partition-congruence work and the1969 congruence-Hecke exposition provide the historical result; later partition-Hecke theory incorporates such progressions.','Publication1967/1969; distinct from the1966Blaricum report.','Established as a published congruence; the numerical audit is explicitly finite.',['Atkin1967_11','Atkin1969','GarvanShomanov2025'],author='A. O. L. Atkin',family='ATKINPART')
C(833,('H63-07',[58,59,60],'49-172'),'T_p; commutation; formes régulières',
 'For integral weightk in modern notation, Tp acts by a(n)↦a(pn)+p^(k−1)a(n/p), with the standard character factor when present. Away from level it preserves cusp forms and the commuting self-adjoint operators admit a simultaneous eigenbasis overC. The manuscript’s dimension−k convention must be converted before using the exponent. A weakly holomorphic modular function with a pole has unbounded pole order under iteratedTp, so its Tp-stable span cannot be finite-dimensional inside a fixed bounded-pole space.',
 'proof sketched','The coefficient action and multiplicativity are checked by formal index manipulation. Pole order grows aspm underTp because the leading pole term cannot cancel against lower-pole summands. This argument does not rule outp-adic limits with varying coefficients.',
 'E','Classical Hecke theory, restated precisely inGuerzhoy2006 eq(2). Thep-adic caveat is essential to the later conjectures.','Known before1967/1969 exposition.','Established with level/character conventions stated. The sentence that any fixedp-adic limit would force a complex identity overreaches unless a common algebraic coefficient ring is fixed.',['Atkin1969','Guerzhoy2006','Serre1973'],author='A. O. L. Atkin',family='ATKINJ')
C(834,('H63-07',[61,62],'180-257'),'Conjecture1; Conjecture2; formes nouvelles',
 'Conjecture1 asks for the normalizedj coefficient series after exactlyα applications ofUp to satisfy the stated recurrence and multiplicativity modulo p^α forp≤23, with the displayed valuation normalizations atsmallp. Conjecture2 replaces one eigenline by two conjugate eigenlines at29/31. The later pages propose old/new decompositions onΓ0(p), including aUp eigenvalue±p^(k/2−1) in modern weightk for a newform of trivial character andprime level.',
 'open on the pages for congruence conjectures; old/new framework announced','The U29 matrix certificate is C-SA16-829. The coefficient recurrences are checked symbolically; the full newform theory is a later theorem and is not re-proved here.',
 'C','AtkinLehner1970 develops the old/new decomposition. Guerzhoy2006/2010 proves substantial congruence forms but the exact iteration thresholds must be compared prime byprime. The p29/31 all-level decomposition is not certified merely by finding a finite-field diagonalization.','Atkin1969; Atkin–Lehner1970; Guerzhoy2006,2010.','Separate established newform theory from the remaining exact-quantifier reconstruction of the conjectures.',['Atkin1969','AtkinLehner1970','Guerzhoy2006','Guerzhoy2010'],author='A. O. L. Atkin',family='ATKINJ')
C(835,('H63-07',[63,64],'263-292'),'Conjecture3 ... unique Fα ... α+β',
 'For a specifiedprimep, the page asks for compatible normalized integral weakly holomorphic functions Fα onΓ0(p), holomorphic onH, with zero at∞ and pole only at0, satisfying Hecke congruences modulo p^α; it further asks that every admissibleF enter the one-dimensional congruence direction afterα+β Up iterations for a fixedβ dependingonF. Literal uniqueness of actual functionsFα is false: atp13, replacingFα byFα+13^αg² withg=(η(13τ)/η(τ))² preserves all residue conditions, normalization, and pole restrictions but changes the function.',
 'false as stated for literal uniqueness; uniform fixed-β convergence is open on the pages','The counterexample usesg=q+O(q²), aΓ0(13) modular function with no interior pole, zero at∞ and pole at0. Thusg² has no q term and13^αg² changes nothing modulo13^α, including compatibility between consecutive levels. Refined statement C-SA16-836.',
 'C','Guerzhoy2006 Thm1 gives∀α∃M(α) and sufficient pole hypotheses; this alone does not giveM(α)≤α+β or literal uniqueness. The residue-class formulation is the appropriate correction.','1969conjecture;2006eventual theorem.','The literal uniqueness assertion is refuted. The refined normalized inverse-limit/eigenline statement and rates need separate hypotheses.',['Atkin1969','Guerzhoy2006','Maier2007'],author='A. O. L. Atkin',family='ATKINJ')
C(836,('H63-07',[63,64],'263-292'),'Fα≡Fα−1 modp^(α−1)',
 'Refinement of C-SA16-835: uniqueness can only mean a normalized residue class modulo p^α or a normalized eigenvector in an explicitly specified inverse-limit module. Eventual convergence∀α∃M(α) does not logically imply a common additive boundM(α)≤α+β; a separate quantitative contraction estimate is required. Integral bases for general congruence subgroups also require an appropriate coefficient ring/cusp parameter rather than an unqualifiedZ basis.',
 'open on the pages, reconstructed with corrected quantifiers','The nonimplication is elementary: a permissible thresholdM(α)=2α has noα-independent additiveβ. This is a logical boundary, not a counterexample to a sharper modular theorem. The explicit nonuniqueness example isC-SA16-835.',
 'C','Serre/Katzp-adic modular forms andGuerzhoy’s exact theorem supply the module setting and eventual congruences. No opened source in this run proves the unrestricted fixed-β statement exactly as written.','Serre1973,Katz1973;Guerzhoy2006.','Partly developed; the dossier leaves the precise stronger theorem as a scoped problem, not as a universal unresolved claim about allp-adic forms.',['Serre1973','Katz1973','Guerzhoy2006'],author='A. O. L. Atkin',family='ATKINJ')

# Miscellaneous copied texts and Grothendieck's own Hodge/local-moduli calculations.
C(837,('H63-08/K63-02',[78,74,76],'H63-08:12-18;K63-02:69-83,123-144'),'les deux théorèmes de changement de base; dualité',
 'The reused SGA4 preface describes étale cohomology, proper/smooth base change, compact supports and Poincaré duality, the Picard-number boundρ≤b2 and Néron–Severi finiteness. Its historical footnote leaves Frobenius integrality/purity outside the results then proved. Crossed-out sheets here are reused paper; their crossing is not evidence that the mathematical theorems were withdrawn.',
 'stated without proof: reused preface/catalogue, not an elliptic-curve proof','No numerical calculation is present. The result hypotheses are supplied by the cited SGA expositions, not by the short preface. The finite-field Frobenius assertions require their own later theorems.',
 'A','SGA4 XII–XVI treats base change andXVII–XVIII compact supports/duality; Deligne’s integrality theorem isSGA7II XXI Appendix, and the Weil-conjecture purity theorem isDeligne1974 Thm1.6. These later results are not all byGrothendieck.','Seminar1963–1964, publicationsSGA4 1972–1973; SGA7II1973; WeilI1974.','Established with the exact smoothness, properness and prime-to-characteristic coefficient hypotheses of the sources.',['SGA4','SGA7II','Deligne1974'],author='SGA4 editorial text / Grothendieck and collaborators',family='SGA')
C(838,('H63-08',[80,81],'table and accompanying comments'),'il y en aurait encore un dixième',
 'There are exactly nine imaginary quadratic fields of class number1, with fundamental discriminants−3,−4,−7,−8,−11,−19,−43,−67,−163. The possible tenth field mentioned in the historical text does not exist. The discriminant parameter is not uniformly prime: the−4 and−8 cases already require care with the page’s d convention.',
 'open on the pages as to a hypothetical tenth field','c63_numerics.py enumerates reduced primitive positive definite binary quadratic forms for the listed discriminants; this checks their class numbers, not the global nonexistence theorem.',
 'B','Heegner1952 gave the original proof with a gap later clarified; Baker/Stark resolved the field class-number-one problem in the1960s. Stark1969 explicitly fills the gap inHeegner’s argument.','Heegner1952; Stark1967; gap clarification receivedJune1968,published1969.','Settled. The page preserves a historical state of knowledge, not a current open problem.',['Stark1967','Stark1969'],author='Copied CM table / annotating hand not identified',family='CM')
C(839,('H63-08',[80,81],'CM j-value table'),'−3,−12,−27; −7,−28; −4,−16',
 'The thirteen imaginary quadratic orders of class number1 have discriminants−3,−4,−7,−8,−11,−12,−16,−19,−27,−28,−43,−67,−163. Their singular moduli are respectively0,1728,−3375,8000,−32768,54000,287496,−884736,−12288000,16581375,−884736000,−147197952000,−262537412640768000, in that order.',
 'stated without proof','c63_numerics.py recomputes the exact integer expressions and reduced-form counts; high-precisionq-expansions give an independent numerical check of every table entry with explicit error/truncation bounds. Numerical agreement alone is not the integrality theorem.',
 'E','Classical complex multiplication computes these ring-class-one singular moduli; the field classification closes the possible extra-field caveat.','Classical table; complete field classification1960s.','Established; distinguish maximal-order fields from nonmaximal orders and discriminant conventions.',['Stark1967','Stark1969','DLMF23Modular'],author='Copied CM table',family='CM')
C(840,('H63-09/K63-01',[87,88,89,90,91,92,93,94,36,37,38,39],'H63-09:1-354;K63-01:1-273'),'formulaire; changements de variables; t²+t',
 'The two additional formularies repeat Weierstrass invariants, quadratic/quartic/sextic twists and characteristic2/3 normal forms. Equalj over a field gives a separable isomorphism after an extension of degree at most24, with the degree depending on the automorphism case. The small-characteristic automorphism equations are s4+s=0 andt²+t=s6 (equivalentlys3 ons∈F4), not the mistranscribed equations listed in the readings.',
 'false as stated at the enumerated polynomial signs and missing factors; the classification structure is correct','c63_algebra.py andc63_more_algebra.py re-evaluate all invariant and automorphism identities. Each repeated wrong formula has its own scan/line reading alarm; neither parallel copy is silently used to alter the other.',
 'E','Tate/Deligne formularies and the standard automorphism/twist classification are the exact family, not an independent unpublishedGrothendieck theorem.','Undated copies; Deligne1968/1975 comparison.','Established after the listed algebraic corrections. The ground-field degree claim is separated from the false common-subgroup claimC-SA16-841.',['Tate1975','Deligne1975'],author='Tate/Serre-related formulary; exact translator uncertain',family='DELIGNE')
C(841,('K63-01',[40],'274-277'),'tout groupe d’automorphismes ... sous-groupe ... 24',
 'If the phrase means every elliptic automorphism group embeds in the characteristic2 groupG24=SL2(F3), it is false: the characteristic3 supersingular groupC3⋊C4 has order12, butSL2(F3) has no subgroup of index2. A mere bound on group order, or on the degree of a suitable isomorphism extension, is a different and valid statement.',
 'false as stated under the common-group interpretation; wording remains a scan question','c63_algebra.py enumerates all24 matrices ofSL2(F3), computes its derived subgroup of order8 and abelianization of order3. An order12 subgroup would be index2, normal and give aC2 quotient, impossible. The characteristic3 group of order12 is explicitly constructed in c63_more_algebra.py.',
 'E','The correct characteristic-dependent automorphism groups are classical and printed inDeligne1975 §2. The contradiction is established independently here.','Classical; corrected here in2026.','The subgroup reading is refuted with every group-order hypothesis checked; retain only the properly formulated degree/order bound.',['Deligne1975'],author='Unattributed formulary annotation',family='DELIGNE')
C(842,('K63-01',[66],'279-335'),'y²=x³+γ4x+γ6; dx=2yω',
 'For a complex elliptic curve with nonzero differentialω and origin, the normalized short Weierstrass coordinates arex=℘Λ(z),y=℘Λ′(z)/2 andω=dz. Thenγ4=−g2(Λ)/4,γ6=−g3(Λ)/4,η=xω, anddx=2yω. The symbolsγ4,γ6 on this handwritten page are short-equation coefficients, not the earlier formularies’c4,c6.',
 'proof sketched; coefficient blanks left on the page','Substitute the differential equation(℘′)²=4℘³−g2℘−g3. Laurent coefficients and the differential relation are checked in c63_more_algebra.py; the y-series must not count its leading−z^−3 term twice.',
 'E','DLMF23.3 gives the differential equation and Laurent normalization; Katz1976 §1.2 uses the identical deRham basis.','Classical before these notes; Katz1976 later comparison.','Established with full-period lattice convention.',['DLMF23Weierstrass','Katz1976'],family='HODGE')
C(843,('K63-01',[67,69,71],'339-354;385-423;442-502'),'η+αω=βω̄',
 'Withη=℘(z)dz and positively oriented full periodsω1,ω2,τ=ω2/ω1, the Hodge-splitting coefficient isα=−(ω̄1η2−ω̄2η1)/(ω̄1ω2−ω̄2ω1)=π²E2*(τ)/(3ω1²), whereE2*=E2−3/(πImτ). Thusαω² is intrinsic. The coefficientα on these pages is the shift coefficient; it is not the sameα used later for the matrix of complex conjugation.',
 'proof sketched; the final expanded lattice sum contains algebra errors','c63_hodge.py derives the ratio from two period equations, uses the Legendre relation, numerically compares the absolutely convergent quasi-period sum to the E2* formula, and symbolically locates each residual in the expanded expression.',
 'B','Katz1976 §1.3, especiallyLemma1.3.6, identifies the same antiholomorphic line via his real-analytic weight2 formS. The match is made after normalization/sign conversion; the notation is not assumed identical.','Katz paper1976; archival dateundetermined.','Established after correcting the explicit lattice-sum algebra.',['Katz1976','DLMF23Weierstrass'],family='HODGE')
C(844,('K63-01',[67],'349-354'),'quand α est-il nul ?',
 'For the canonical short-Weierstrass second-kind classη=℘dz, the Hodge shiftα vanishes exactly whenτ isSL2(Z)-equivalent toi or exp(2πi/3); equivalentlyj=1728 or0. Changingη by an arbitrary multiple ofω would change this question, so the canonicalη is essential.',
 'open on the pages','An independent proof is given in work/parts/cote63/hodge_proof.md: modularity gives the two elliptic zeros; on the standard fundamental domain the imaginary-part sine-series bound forcesReτ=0 or±1/2, and an explicit derivative bound gives uniqueness on each boundary line. c63_hodge.py certifies the rational inequalities and numerical normalizations.',
 'B','The problem is the zero locus of the classical completed weight2 Eisenstein series, not the infinitely many zeros of holomorphicE2. Katz1976 supplies its modular/Hodge identification; this run independently proves the zero-locus statement from thatq-expansion. No claim of a newly discovered theorem is made.','Classical modular form; independent check19November2026.','Closed by the displayed proof with no conjectural transcendence input.',['Katz1976'],family='HODGE')
C(845,('K63-01',[67],'349-354'),'α algébrique ? ... multiplication complexe',
 'Fix an elliptic curve and differential defined overQbar, and use its canonical short-Weierstrass deRham basis. ForCM curves the antiholomorphic line is defined over a number field containing the endomorphisms, so the shift coefficientα is algebraic. The converse asks whether an algebraic splitting of the complex Hodge decomposition in algebraic deRham cohomology forcesCM. Without the algebraic model/differential normalization, arbitrary rescaling makes a bare algebraicity question ill-posed.',
 'open on the pages','The CM direction is re-proved by the distinct eigenvalues of a nonrational endomorphism onH1dR and its algebraic eigenprojector. No numerical evaluation proves the converse, and Betti rationality is not substituted for algebraic deRham rationality.',
 'C','Katz1976 Lemma4.0.7 proves theCM direction; Question4.0.8 onp501 states this exact converse. Targeted later searches are recorded; no opened source proving the converse was identified. Schneider’s theorem about algebraicτ andj does not by itself answer this question.','CM theorem older; exact Katz question1976; status searchNovember2026.','CM direction established; converse retained as an unresolved/uncertified exact question with medium confidence on exhaustive current status.',['Katz1976','MOHodgeCM2010'],confidence='medium',family='HODGE')
C(846,('K63-01',[69,71],'385-423;442-502'),'η1=−3/ω1+ω1²Σ ...',
 'The absolutely convergent lattice formulas for second-kind periods areηi=−3/ωi+ωi²Σa∈Λ\{0,ωi}1/[a²(a−ωi)]. Writinga=mω1+nω2 fixes the repeatedω1 typo in scan69. Combining the two sums gives the ratio inC-SA16-843, but the later τ-expression loses a factor3 and changes signs in two numerator terms.',
 'corrected by himself later for the index exclusions; false as stated in the final combined algebra','c63_hodge.py compares rectangular partial sums with the independentq-series, records the truncation error, and computes exact rational residuals between the uncombined and combined summands. The factor3 and signs are not guessed from numerical agreement.',
 'E','The Weierstrass zeta/quasi-period identities are classical and occur in Katz1976 §1.2. The particular expanded expression is the manuscript’s calculation and is corrected here.',
 'Classical identities; manuscriptundated.','The convergent formula is correct; the listed algebraic alternatives await inspection of the ink.',['Katz1976','DLMF23Weierstrass'],family='HODGE')
C(847,('K63-02',[73,75,77],'5-65;88-118;147-158'),'β+β̄=0; 4ρσ=1−αᾱ',
 'ForV=L⊕L^−1 with its normalized real-compatible alternating pairing, a conjugate-linear involution withβ≠0 is determined byα,β; compatibility forcesβ purely imaginary. Withβ=−2iρ,ρ>0 andσ=−(2i)^−1∫y∧ȳ, its matrix gives4ρσ=|α|²−1. The page has the opposite sign. No general bound|α|≤1 follows: for the actual elliptic latticeτ=3i in the canonical basis,α=−πImτ·E2*(τ)/3 has|α|>1.',
 'false as stated: sign identity and ensuing bound','c63_algebra.py checks the conjugation matrix. Atτ=i,α0,ρ1,σ−1/4 contradict the printed sign. Atτ=3i, c63_hodge.py computesα≈1−π with a certified tail bound, contradicting|α|≤1. The square-lattice matrix satisfiesM·M̄=I and preserves the pairing.',
 'E','This is elementary polarized linear algebra; Katz1976 §1.3 supplies the normalization bridge to actual elliptic curves. The erroneous bound is not a consequence ofHodge positivity, which has opposite signatures on the twoHodge lines.','Classical linear algebra; corrected in this run.','Refuted with explicit elliptic and linear-algebra counterexamples.',['Katz1976'],family='HODGE')
C(848,('K63-02',[77],'156-158'),'(arg α)^6 ... fonction de j',
 'Where the real-structure coefficientα≠0, choosingω withω^12=Δ leaves aμ12 ambiguity;α transforms by the square of that phase, so(α/|α|)^6 is choice-independent. It is a real-analytic modular invariant away from its zero locus, not thereby a holomorphic or algebraic rational function ofj. The modulus and volume quantities also depend on fixing the indicated discriminant normalization.',
 'open on the pages as to an explicit formula in j','Theμ12-toμ6 phase transformation is checked directly in c63_hodge.py. TheE2* formula gives an explicit real-analytic expression after choosingτ, but does not make the function holomorphic in j.',
 'C','Katz1976 identifies the underlying real-analytic weight2 form; almost-holomorphic CM-value theory treats related modular invariants. No opened source was found giving exactly this normalized phase function in the manuscript’s notation.','Katz1976; laterCM-value work; search2026.','The invariance is proved; a useful global formula/extension at zeros is a scoped remaining task.',['Katz1976','DoseEtAl2015'],confidence='medium',family='HODGE')
C(849,('K63-02',[96],'189-229'),'3 div(c4);2 div(c6);72;32',
 'On the smooth elliptic moduli stack, j=c4³/Δ andj−1728=c6²/Δ, hence the zero divisors are3V(c4) and2V(c6). OverZ[1/6], V(c4)≅Bμ6 andV(c6)≅Bμ4 after choosing the standardCM models. Their torsor classes overZ[1/6] number72 and32 becausePicZ[1/6]=0 and units are±2^a3^b; H¹(μn)=units/(units)^n.',
 'stated without proof; stack/classifying-space interpretation reconstructed explicitly','c63_algebra.py computes2·6²=72 and2·4²=32; the Kummer exact sequence andPic=0 give the count, not mere enumeration of coefficients. The divisor multiplicities follow from the invariant identity withΔ a unit.',
 'E','Classical twist classification and theKummer sequence; Deligne1975 supplies automorphism groups andj. TheBμn notation is a modern reconstruction of the page’s isomorphism-type computation.','Classical; Deligne1968/1975.','Established overZ[1/6]; changing the base changes bothPic andunit factors.',['Deligne1975','StacksKummer'],family='LOCAL')
C(850,('K63-02',[96,99],'231-244;277-298'),'anneau versel W(k)[[t]] ... équations de c4,c6',
 'At a supersingular elliptic curve over a perfect fieldk of characteristicp, the deformation functor with identified special fiber has a one-parameter smooth Witt-vector hullW(k)[[t]]. Atp2 one may usey²+txy+y=x³, givingc4=t(t³−24),c6=−t6+36t3−216,Δ=t³−27. Atp3 usey²=x³+t x²−x, givingc4=16(t²+3),c6=−32t(2t²+9),Δ=16(t²+4). This is a deformation ring, not the coarsej-line completed ring with inertia forgotten.',
 'open on the pages: request for explicit equations','All invariant polynomials are computed exactly in c63_algebra.py. Their reducedHasse parameters t and4t give nonzero tangent coordinates; deformation unobstructedness gives versality. The chosen special fibers are smooth becauseΔ is a unit.',
 'B','Serre–Tate deformation theory and the universal Weierstrass presentation establish the one-dimensional hull. Katz1981 Thm1.2.1 is the precise deformation comparison. The displayed coordinates are reconstructed and verified here.','Serre–Tate1960s; Katz1981 exposition.','The requested local equations are supplied. A quotient by the automorphism action must be retained for the stack.',['Katz1981SerreTate','Deligne1975'],family='LOCAL')
C(851,('K63-02',[96,98,99,100],'231-244;247-298;318-327'),'multiplicités4,2; tangentes distinctes ... sont égales',
 'In mixed characteristic2 on the full-level3 moduli chart, the four branches ofV(c4) through the supersingular point have four distinct tangent directions. InW(F4)[[t]], chooseu withu³=3; then c4=t(t−2u)(t−2uζ)(t−2uζ²). Modulo m², m=(2,t), their equations aret,t−2,t−2ζ,t−2ζ². Their slopes are the four elements ofF4. The scan100 assertion that the tangents are equal is therefore false; the earlier scan98 question had the correct alternative.',
 'corrected by himself later, but the later correction is false as stated','c63_more_algebra.py verifies the factorization and pairwise nonzero differences inF4. Hensel’s lemma givesu≡1mod2; Δ≡1modm, so the example is an elliptic deformation. Prime-to2 full level3 lifts uniquely étale-locally, preserving this completed ring. An independent Hesse-family calculation gives the same four lines.',
 'B','Hua2026 §18 explicitly gives a Hesse-chart counterexample to the same later assertion; his proof assumes the Hesse-moduli identification andc4 formula. ArtebaniDolgachev2009 supplies that family. This run’s Weierstrass calculation is independent of the claimed Lean certificate.','Hua preprintNovember2026; Hesse theoryclassical.','The retraction is refuted. No scan reading is silently changed; handwritten assertion and mathematical truth are separately recorded.',['Hua2026','ArtebaniDolgachev2009','Katz1981SerreTate'],family='LOCAL')
C(852,('K63-02',[98,100],'270-272;318-327'),'G′ ... V1×V2 ... fixe chaque composante',
 'The action of the order24 groupSL2(F3) on the local level3 geometry has central±1 acting ineffectively on the deformation base. Its effectiveA4 quotient acts on the four branches asF4⋊F4×. The asserted invariant horizontal/vertical splitting of the mixed-characteristic cotangent space is false: in a Hesse coordinate the involutiont↦(t+2)/(t−1) induces2↦2,t↦t+2 modulo(2,t)², a nontrivial transvection. Stabilizing one branch does not mean fixing allfour.',
 'false as stated in the splitting argument supporting the tangent retraction','c63_more_algebra.py checks the rational map, involution and cotangent congruence. The action’s nonzerooff-diagonal term gives the exact failed premise; group-order counting alone could not decide tangency.',
 'B','Hua2026 §18 gives the same finite local obstruction, with the identification assumptions stated. The explicit level3 family and itsHessian group are treated byArtebaniDolgachev2009.','ClassicalHesse group; exact archive comparisonNovember2026.','The splitting argument is refuted. The coarse/stack/kernel distinctions explain why a naïvely faithfulSL2(F3) action on the base is misleading.',['Hua2026','ArtebaniDolgachev2009'],family='LOCAL')
C(853,('K63-02',[100],'327-351'),'une action de μ3 ... centraliser G′',
 'The generic μ3 automorphisms of the universal elliptic curve over the four-branch c4=0 locus cannot extend as a single automorphism over the glued base at2: any extension specializes to an automorphism acting trivially on all four deformation branches. The branch action has kernel±1 insideSL2(F3), so an order3 element cannot qualify. The page’s G-equivariant obstruction also follows fromZ(SL2(F3))=C2. This concerns automorphisms of the universal curve over its fixed base, not arbitrary order3 automorphisms of the base scheme.',
 'proof sketched, with the universal-family/base distinction essential','c63_more_algebra.py computes the kernel ofSL2(F3) on its four projective lines. An automorphism of the whole universal family must preserve each branch deformation; their stabilizers intersect in±1. Group computation and deformation interpretation are separate check events.',
 'E','Elementary group theory and universal deformation, in theHesse/modular setting ofArtebaniDolgachev2009.','Classical group computation; manuscriptundated.','The obstruction is established for the universal curve over the fixed glued base; it does not forbid the visible order3 symmetries of the Hesse parameter line.',['ArtebaniDolgachev2009','Katz1981SerreTate'],family='LOCAL')
C(854,('K63-01',[68,70,72],'360-379;433-435;512-532'),'U(λ*μ)=U(λ)U(μ); inverses',
 'For a strongly continuous representation of a locally compact group on a suitable complete locally convex space, integration against compactly supported finite measures gives operatorsU(λ) withU(λ*μ)=U(λ)U(μ), when the integrals andFubini are valid. In a unital Banach algebra, theNeumann criterion gives an open unit group and continuous inversion; a norm-convergent sequence of units with uniformly bounded inverses has an invertible limit. The variant using the undefined symbolD requiresD to be the spectral radius and the commuting hypotheses from the missing previous proposition.',
 'proved there for the Banach norm argument; integral representation proof sketched; spectral-radius context incomplete','The Neumann-series proof and bounded-inverse limit estimate are independently rechecked in hodge_proof.md under the reconstructedBanach hypotheses. No claim is made that the truncated typescript supplies all locally convex integration assumptions.',
 'E','StandardBanach-algebra/representation theory before the notes; these are reused typescript fragments, not elliptic-curve constructions. The missing definition ofD is recorded asF-level provenance uncertainty within this grouped record.','Classical; exact typescriptdateunknown.','Norm version established; the wider locally convex/spectral-radius statement is conditional on explicitly stated reconstruction.',['BanachAlgebraSource'],confidence='medium',author='Reused analysis typescript, author uncertain',family='REUSED')
C(855,('K63-02',[101],'354-369'),'spécialisation ... équivalence rationnelle',
 'The crossed fragment concerns a cycle-specialization operation and asks descent modulo rational equivalence subject to a definability condition not fully specified on the page. A standard modern refinement is specializationCH*(Xη)→CH*(Xs) for a smooth proper family over aDVR, compatible with intersection products. The page alone does not determine a theorem for an arbitrary specialization of points or arbitrary singular family.',
 'open on the pages; crossed reused fragment, not a proved or disproved general theorem','No numerical computation occurs. The modern refinement is checked against theDVR construction via closure and Gysin pullback; missing setup is not filled in as if it were the author’s text.',
 'F','Fulton’s specialization construction establishes the precisely statedDVR refinement, but the fragment’s original unrestricted scope is not identifiable.','Modernreference1984/1998; archivaldateunknown.','Original formulation notidentifiable; useful standard refinement stated separately.',['FultonIntersection','StacksSpecialization'],confidence='medium',author='Crossed specialization fragment',family='REUSED')
C(856,('H63-08/K63-02',[83,84,85,97],'H63-08:161-239;K63-02:233-244'),'bibliographie; Carlos Ruiz S.,9mars1968',
 'The bibliography and the reused letter are provenance evidence. The letter is addressed toGrothendieck and dated9March1968; its mathematical request about exterior-power operations is the correspondent’s, not a provedGrothendieck result. The Deuring bibliography entry conflates the titleDie Typen derMultiplikatorenringe with a1949 locator; the checked title belongs toHamburg14(1941),197–272.',
 'stated without proof: metadata, not a research claim','No mathematical computation. Exact dates/titles are checked against the transcribed letter and opened bibliography records; a crossed sheet is not evidence of mathematical withdrawal.',
 'F','The letter’s further discussion cannot be identified without its surrounding correspondence. Deuring’s publication metadata is independently recoverable fromSerre1973’s bibliography and publisher records.','Letter9March1968; Deuring1941.','Keep as provenance and a bounded reading/bibliography correction, outside the elliptic-claim originality counts.',['Serre1973','Deuring1941'],confidence='medium',author='Correspondent/bibliography compiler',family='REUSED')

C(857,('K63-02',[96,99],'215-229;277-298'),'M′ régulier enp3; M″ nonrégulier enp2,3',
 'On the deformation stack, the fiber multiplicities ofV(c4) are4 atp2 and2 atp3; those ofV(c6) are6 atp2 and3 atp3. The total divisorV(c4) is nonregular at2 but regular at3; V(c6) is nonregular atboth. This concerns the divisor in a two-dimensional mixed-characteristic deformation chart, not merely its reduced special fiber.',
 'stated without proof after explicit regularity questions','UseC-SA16-850: mod2,c4=t4 andc6=t6; mod3,c4=t² andc6 is a unit times t³. InW(k)[[t]],c4 atp3 is16(t²+3), outside m², so its quotient is regular. Atp2,c4∈m4 andc6∈m3; atp3,c6∈m3, so these hypersurfaces are nonregular. c63_more_algebra.py records the exact orders.',
 'B','The Weierstrass deformation presentation and hypersurface regularity criterion establish the assertions. Hua2026 §18 preserves multiplicity4/nonregularity while refuting the tangent retraction.','Classical deformation algebra; exact reconstruction checked2026.','All four fiber multiplicities and all three regularity judgments are confirmed.',['Deligne1975','Katz1981SerreTate','Hua2026'],family='LOCAL')
C(858,('K63-02',[98,99],'247-268;302-315'),'4 copies de μ3*; bonne réduction ... (ni3 ?)',
 'After adjoiningζ and inverting3, thej0 level3 locus has four components, each a copy ofSpecZ[ζ,1/3], meeting overthe uniqueF4 point at2. OverZ[1/6] it is the inducedGL2(F3)-spaceμ3*×^B G, withB aBorel oforder12 andindex4. The chosenj0 curve can be taken asy²+y=x³ with full3-torsion overQ(ζ); it has good reduction away3, including2. The parenthetical suggestion ofgood reduction also at3 is false for this curve.',
 'proof sketched for component decomposition; good reduction at3 left as a question','c63_more_algebra.py countsGL2(F3)=48,B=12,SL2(F3)=24,B′=6 andfour line cosets. The3-division polynomial has roots0 andcube roots of−1 overQ(ζ). Δ=−27 has valuation6 at the ramified prime above3; every admissible equation change alters this by a multiple of12. A good-reduction equation ofvaluation0 is impossible.',
 'E','TheHesse level3 model gives the four components andgood reduction away3; minimal-discriminant invariance answers the parenthetical question.','Classical Hesse and minimal-model theory; checked2026.','The decomposition andgood reduction away3 are confirmed; the extra3 claim is refuted for the displayed universalCM curve.',['ArtebaniDolgachev2009','Tate1975'],family='LOCAL')

